//
// `null(<reason>)` in an interpolation names the fault that HAPPENED, not the fault the op
// it passed through could have had.  loft#1169.
//
// The tag is armed at PARSE time from the op's shape — `OpTagFault(kind)` is emitted right
// before each fault-prone `*Nullable` peer in format scope — and consumed at RUN time from
// the VALUE, by whichever format op meets the type's null sentinel.  Two facts have to meet
// for it to be true: *this op could fault* and *this op did fault*.  Only the first was ever
// checked, so any null that merely FLOWED THROUGH the op wore its name:
//
//   * `{v[1]}` on a `vector<integer?>` whose element is genuinely null said `null(oob)` —
//     the read succeeded, the index was in range, and the null was the element's own;
//   * `{n / a}` with a null dividend and `a == 5` said `null(/0)`, with no zero anywhere.
//
// A and B below were then indistinguishable from their real-fault siblings, and so were C
// and D — the tag carried no information at all on these shapes, which is worse than having
// none.  Both backends agreed, so nothing differential could see it.
//
// `OpTagFault` now only ARMS the hole and `Stores::note_format_fault` is the one place a
// cause is written; every fault-prone peer calls it with its own answer, so a tag appears
// exactly where a fault happened.
//
// ⚠ A peer that did NOT fault leaves the tag ALONE rather than clearing it, and that is the
// whole reason this is a set and not a keep-or-clear.  One hole can hold several fault-prone
// ops while only the outermost is armed, so a clearing peer erases a cause an inner op just
// recorded: the first version of this fix cleared, and `{v[0] / z}` — a genuine division by
// zero after a successful read — lost its `/0`.  Leaving it also makes `{v[9] / 2}` report
// the OVERRUN that actually produced its null, which no build before this one did.
//
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   the runtime LOG is unchanged and not re-measured here.  The
//                        non-`Nullable` ops raise `DivideByZero` only for a real zero
//                        divisor and the peers never raise at all, so `@FR-E-Report` was
//                        already right; this is the render path alone.
//
// ⚠ Two neighbouring cells the walk measured, and what each turned out to be — recorded
// because both READ like defects and only one was:
//   * `5.0 / 0.0` renders `inf`, not `null(/0)`.  That is DELIBERATE, not a bug: the float
//     null is a NaN and `1.0 / 0.0` is a representable IEEE value, not a missing one
//     (loft#983 reverted forcing NaN, which had made one expression answer `inf` inline and
//     `null` once bound, and made `a / b ?? 0.0` guard nothing).  `0.0 / 0.0` and `5.0 % 0.0`
//     ARE null, and are asserted below.  `(E-Uncomp)` did not carry that carve-out and now
//     does — the same shape as the rule this walk started from.
//   * `"hi"[9]` used to render `null(oob)` on `--interpret` and EMPTY on `--native`.  Fixed
//     here, toward NATIVE: `@FR-F-Render` says a null character renders as nothing, and says
//     why — iterating text past its end must append no garbage — so the interpreter's extra
//     was the deviation.  Asserted below on both backends.
//
// @falsified-at: 48544f1e — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0, native
// exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0.  The count is 1 because a failed assert stops
// the run, so the cells were measured against the control SEPARATELY: all seven inherited-null
// cells flip (`v[1]`, `v[n]`, `n / a`, `a / n`, `n % a`, and both two-hole strings), and the
// three real-fault cells read the SAME on both builds — which is the property that matters,
// because a fix that just stopped tagging would also pass every cell above them.
//   HOLDS: leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees. Falsify prints
//   only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on
//   both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.
fn main() {
  v: vector<integer?> = [1, null, 3];
  n: integer? = null;
  a: integer = 5;
  z: integer = 0;

  // ── a null that PASSED THROUGH the op renders bare ───────────────────────────
  assert("{v[1]}" == "null", "an in-range element that is itself null");
  assert("{v[n]}" == "null", "a null INDEX is an inherited null, not an overrun");
  assert("{n / a}" == "null", "a null dividend, with the divisor 5");
  assert("{a / n}" == "null", "a null divisor is inherited too, not a division by zero");
  assert("{n % a}" == "null", "a null dividend under %");

  // ── a fault that actually HAPPENED keeps its name ────────────────────────────
  assert("{v[9]}" == "null(oob)", "a real overrun still says oob");
  assert("{a / z}" == "null(/0)", "a real divide by zero still says /0");
  assert("{a % z}" == "null(%0)", "a real modulo by zero still says %0");

  // A null CHARACTER renders as nothing whatever produced it (@FR-F-Render), so a text
  // overrun carries no tag on either backend — this is the cell that used to disagree.
  assert("[{\"hi\"[9]}]" == "[]", "a text overrun renders nothing, both backends");
  assert("{5.0 / 0.0}" == "inf", "float /0 is a representable IEEE value, not a null");
  assert("{0.0 / 0.0}" == "null", "but 0.0/0.0 IS the float null");
  assert("{5.0 % 0.0}" == "null", "and so is a float modulo by zero");

  // ── one hole, several fault-prone ops ───────────────────────────────────────
  // Only the OUTERMOST op is armed, so a peer that CLEARED on not-faulting would erase a
  // cause an inner op had just recorded.  That is why a peer only ever SETS: a null that
  // was inherited keeps the cause of wherever it was born.
  w: vector<integer> = [10, 20, 30];
  assert("{w[0] / z}" == "null(/0)", "a real /0 after a SUCCESSFUL inner read");
  assert("{(w[0] + 1) / z}" == "null(/0)", "and with an arithmetic step between them");
  assert("{w[9] / 2}" == "null(oob)", "the inner OVERRUN is the cause, not the division");
  assert("{w[0] / n}" == "null", "and an inherited null with no fault anywhere is bare");

  // Arming is what keeps this to format scope: the same peers are emitted for a `??`
  // discharge, and a fault there must leave nothing behind for an unrelated hole.
  discharged: integer = a / z ?? 7;
  assert(discharged == 7, "the discharge itself still works");
  assert("{n}" == "null", "a guarded fault leaves no cause for the next hole to wear");

  // ── and an ordinary value is untouched by any of it ──────────────────────────
  assert("{v[0]}" == "1", "an ordinary element");
  assert("{a / 5}" == "1", "an ordinary division");
  assert("{a % 5}" == "0", "an ordinary modulo");
  assert("{\"hi\"[0]}" == "h", "an ordinary text index");

  // The tag is consumed per hole, so one interpolation's fault cannot colour the next.
  assert("{a / z}{n / a}" == "null(/0)null", "a real fault does not leak into the next hole");
  assert("{n / a}{a / z}" == "nullnull(/0)", "nor does a cleared one suppress the next");

  print("a-fault-tag-names-the-fault-that-happened ok\n");
}
